course: chemistry
lesson: 18
title: The fizz: vinegar, lemon and soda
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Pour vinegar onto a heap of baking soda and it froths up like a witch's cauldron. The acid in the vinegar and the alkali in the soda react and make a new substance, carbon dioxide gas, which bubbles out.
explain: When the fizz stops, one of the two has been used up. Add more of whichever ran out and the fizzing starts again: that is how you can find out which one it was.
explain: Carbon dioxide is invisible but heavier than air. It can fill a balloon that will not float, and a grown-up can even "pour" it from a jug over a candle and put the flame out.
explain: Eggshell and chalk are calcium carbonate, so they fizz in vinegar too, and geologists test rocks with a drop of acid: limestone fizzes. Warm vinegar fizzes harder, because warmth makes particles move faster and meet more often.
explain: The science-fair volcano is great fun, but real volcanoes are completely different: they erupt melted rock pushed out by hot gases from deep underground.
fact: Vinegar (an acid) and baking soda (an alkali) react to make carbon dioxide gas, which makes the fizz.
fact: When the fizzing stops, one of the two has been used up.
fact: Washing-up liquid in a model volcano traps the carbon dioxide in bubbles and makes foam.
fact: Real volcanoes erupt melted rock pushed by hot gases deep underground, not by vinegar and soda.
fact: Carbon dioxide is heavier than air, so a balloon filled with it from the reaction does not float.
fact: Carbon dioxide pushes oxygen away from a flame, so poured over a candle it puts it out; some fire extinguishers use it.
fact: Sherbet has a dry acid and baking soda that only react when wet, so it fizzes in your mouth and not in the packet.
fact: Popping candy works differently: gas is trapped inside hard sugar and pops out as the sugar melts.
fact: Gas made in a closed container pushes on the walls until the weakest part gives, so reacting chemicals in a tightly closed bottle can burst it dangerously.
fact: Eggshell is calcium carbonate; in vinegar it fizzes away over days and leaves a rubbery egg held by its thin skin.
fact: Chalk and limestone are calcium carbonate too and fizz with acid; chalk cliffs are made from the shells of countless tiny sea creatures.
fact: Reactions go faster when warm, because particles move faster and meet more often; the same is why food goes off slower in the fridge.
fact: In a fair test you change only one thing and keep everything else the same, and scientists repeat tests to make sure a result was not luck.
exercise: What gas makes the fizz when vinegar meets baking soda? | Carbon dioxide | Pure oxygen gas | Helium | Hydrogen
exercise: What does it mean when the fizzing stops? | One of the two has been used up | The gas has all turned back into water | The whole mixture has frozen solid | The bubbles are hiding inside the jar
exercise: How could you tell which one ran out first? | Add a bit more of one and see if it fizzes again | Smell the mixture very carefully for a sour smell | Wait a whole day and look at it again | Stir it hard until it fizzes again
exercise: Why does washing-up liquid make the model volcano foam? | It traps the gas in bubbles | It is an acid that adds more gas | It heats the whole mixture up | It makes the vinegar stronger
exercise: Is a baking-soda volcano how a real volcano works? | No, real ones erupt melted rock and hot gases | Yes, real volcanoes are full of vinegar and soda | Yes, exactly the same, just on a much bigger scale | No, real ones are powered by water only
exercise: Why doesn't the balloon filled from the reaction float? | Carbon dioxide is heavier than air | The balloon has a tiny hole in its side | The gas inside is far too cold to rise | Carbon dioxide is lighter than helium
exercise: How can you "pour" a gas you cannot see? | It is heavier than air, so it flows down | It turns into a clear liquid inside the jug | It is pulled along by the candle's magnet | It is pushed out by the warmth of the jug
exercise: Why does the poured gas put the candle out? | It pushes the oxygen away from the flame | It is very cold and freezes the wick solid | It is wet and soaks the candle | It is an alkali that cancels fire out
exercise: Why does sherbet fizz in your mouth but not in the packet? | It needs to be wet to react | The sweets in the packet are too cold | Your teeth crack the bubbles open | The air in your mouth is special
exercise: How is popping candy different from sherbet? | Gas is trapped inside it, not made by a reaction | It contains much more baking soda than sherbet | It reacts with air, not with water | It is made of tiny fireworks
exercise: Why is it dangerous to put reacting chemicals in a tightly closed bottle? | The gas builds up until the bottle bursts | The bottle melts from the heat of the reaction | The chemicals turn into poison | The lid sticks forever
exercise: What is eggshell made of? | Calcium carbonate | Iron oxide | Pure salt | Hard sugar
exercise: Why does an egg left in vinegar for days become rubbery? | The shell dissolves, leaving only the thin skin | The vinegar slowly cooks the egg inside the shell | The egg soaks up oil from the vinegar | The yolk turns into rubber
exercise: How could a drop of vinegar help you identify a rock? | Limestone and chalk fizz with acid | Granite turns pink in vinegar | Every rock fizzes the same | Only metal rocks react
exercise: What are chalk cliffs made from? | Shells of countless tiny sea creatures | Old bones of dinosaurs pressed together | Salt left by dried-up seas | Ash from volcanoes
exercise: Why does warm vinegar fizz harder? | Particles move faster and meet more often | Warm vinegar is a much stronger acid than cold | Heat adds gas to the vinegar | Warmth makes soda bigger
exercise: Why is it unfair to change two things at once in a test? | You can't tell which change caused the result | It takes far too long to finish and write up | It uses up too much vinegar | Scientists are not allowed to
test: Emma's volcano stopped fizzing. She adds more soda and nothing happens. What ran out? | The vinegar | The soda | The washing-up liquid | The air
test: "the science-fair volcano is EXACTLY how Vesuvius works" says Josh. What's true? | Real volcanoes erupt melted rock and gas | He's right, a real one is just a lot bigger | Real volcanoes use lemon juice | Real volcanoes are powered by soap
test: A cartoon shows a balloon filled from vinegar and soda floating to the moon. What would really happen? | It would not float, the gas is heavier than air | It would float just like a helium balloon | It would burst into flames | It would turn into vinegar
test: Liam wants to put the vinegar and soda in a bottle and screw the cap on tight "to see what happens". What's the honest answer? | Don't: the gas builds up and can burst the bottle | Fine, nothing happens in a closed bottle | Fine, the gas just disappears | Fine, it makes the bottle colder only
test: "i put an egg in vinegar for 3 days and now it bounces lol how". Why? | The shell dissolved, only the skin holds it | The vinegar slowly turned the whole egg into rubber | The egg froze in the fridge | The yolk turned into air
test: You find a white rock on a walk. A drop of vinegar makes it fizz. What might it be? | Limestone or chalk | A piece of granite | A lump of gold | Coal
test: If you did the fizz test in fridge-cold vinegar instead of warm vinegar, what would happen? | It would fizz more slowly | It would fizz much, much faster | It would not fizz at all ever | It would freeze into a solid foam
test: Mia tests three amounts of soda, but uses warm vinegar for one and cold for another. What's wrong? | She changed two things, so it isn't fair | Nothing, that's a perfect test of the soda | She should use more soda | She should use lemon not vinegar
test: "sherbet's been in me pocket all day and hasn't fizzed, is it dead?" | No, it only fizzes when wet in your mouth | Yes, sherbet goes off in an hour | Yes, pockets kill sherbet | No, it fizzes only at night
test: Why does a carbon dioxide fire extinguisher help on some fires? | The gas pushes oxygen away from the flames | The gas is wet and soaks the fire like a hose | The gas burns up the flames' fuel | The gas cools the fire with ice
test: Leo does his fizz test once and gets a big result. His teacher says "do it again". Why? | To check the result wasn't just luck | Because the first one was boring | To use up the rest of the vinegar | Because tests must be done in pairs
exam: Identify the gas liberated when an acid such as vinegar reacts with baking soda. | Carbon dioxide | Oxygen | Nitrogen | Hydrogen
exam: It is given that a closed vessel contains reacting substances which produce gas. Ascertain the principal hazard. | The pressure may rise until the vessel bursts | The vessel may become strongly and permanently magnetised | The substances may turn into solid metal | The vessel will become permanently cold
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a fair test? | Only one factor is altered whilst all others are held constant | All factors are altered simultaneously to save time | The experiment is conducted on one occasion only | The result is determined before the experiment begins
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the effect of temperature upon the rate of a reaction. | Warmth increases the rate, as particles move faster and meet more often | Warmth invariably halts the reaction completely and permanently in every case | Temperature has no effect upon the rate of any reaction | Cold invariably accelerates the reaction
